  "sections": [
    {
      "sectionId": "section-introduction",
      "heading": "Introduction",
      "sourceAnchor": "introduction",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Easily overlooked as a "
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "spellcasting feat",
              "href": "https://rpgbot.net/dnd5/characters/feats/spellcasting-feats/"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ", Spell Sniper qualifies since it allows you to select a spell beyond your class features. The primary appeal is to add a good ranged spell attack option for classes that don’t have them, and remove the nuisance of cover for classes like the Warlock which rely heavily on spell attacks."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Because you’re limited to cantrips which make spell attacks, your choices are\n\tvery limited, and very few of the spell options are worth selecting. And,\n\tsince you use the spellcasting ability of the class which take the spell from,\n\tspellcasters are typically locked into classes which share a spellcasting\n\tability, severely limiting Spell Sniper’s usefulness. Even worse, several\n\tspellcasting classes have no qualifying cantrips."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The incredibly narrow function of the feat, the sparse cantrip options, and the situational nature of the feat’s other benefits mean that there is an extremely narrow set of characters who benefit from Spell Sniper in any meaningful way. Warlocks who are heavily reliant on Eldritch Blast will find the cover benefit helpful, and bards and sorcerers will enjoy eldritch blast as a go-to damage cantrip, but that’s basically the extend of Spell Sniper’s useful benefits. Any other characters will be better served by Magic Initiate, and even those classes which do benefit from Spell Sniper are probably still better off with Magic Initiate."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-4",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "For more information about spellcasting feats, including ratings of the various feats, see our "
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "Spellcasting Feats Guide",
              "href": "https://rpgbot.net/dnd5/characters/feats/spellcasting-feats/"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
